# Six Months Into Crypto: What I Wish Someone Had Told Me on Day One

About six months ago I bought my first bit of Bitcoin. A friend kept bringing it up, I got curious, and one night I downloaded an exchange app and bought $50 worth. I didn't really know what I was doing. I'm still learning, but I've picked up enough that I want to start writing it down, partly to keep myself honest and partly in case it helps someone else who is just getting started.

So here's the short list of things I wish I'd known on day one.

## 1. Nobody knows where the price is going

In my first month I watched charts constantly. I read every "this coin is about to explode" post. None of it helped. The people who sound the most certain are usually the ones selling something. Now I check prices way less often, and I'm a lot calmer.

## 2. "Not your keys, not your coins" is a real thing

When your crypto sits on an exchange, the exchange holds it for you. That's convenient, but it means you're trusting them. I didn't understand this at first. Learning about wallets and private keys was the biggest "aha" moment I've had so far (I'll write a whole post on it).

## 3. Fees add up fast

I made a bunch of small buys early on and didn't notice how much I was paying in fees and spreads. Now I look at the total cost before I hit buy, and I make fewer, larger purchases.

## 4. Only put in what you can afford to lose

Everyone says this, and I used to roll my eyes at it. Then I watched my small portfolio drop 30% in a couple of weeks. Because I'd only put in money I was okay losing, it didn't wreck me. It just taught me something.

## 5. Learning is the actual investment

The best thing I've gotten out of crypto so far isn't gains. It's understanding how this stuff works: blockchains, wallets, why people care about decentralization. That knowledge isn't going to drop 30% overnight.

## What's next for this blog

I'm going to keep posting as I learn: what I'm trying, what's confusing me, and mistakes I make along the way. I'm not a financial advisor and I'm definitely not an expert. I'm just a guy figuring it out in public.

If you're new too, welcome. Let's learn together.

